1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,863
<font color="#000000">Soft tree ensembles are gradient-trained decision
trees that rival neural networks on tabular data,</font>

2
00:00:07,943 --> 00:00:10,644
<font color="#000000">yet their training has lacked theory.</font>

3
00:00:10,724 --> 00:00:16,057
<font color="#000000">This ICLR 2022 paper introduces the Tree Neural
Tangent Kernel, or TNTK,</font>

4
00:00:16,137 --> 00:00:20,041
<font color="#000000">describing an ensemble of infinitely many soft trees.</font>

5
00:00:20,121 --> 00:00:24,176
<font color="#000000">With it, the authors prove global training convergence,</font>

6
00:00:24,256 --> 00:00:27,258
<font color="#000000">show oblivious trees match ordinary ones,</font>

7
00:00:27,338 --> 00:00:31,919
<font color="#000000">and reveal that very deep trees degenerate into a
flat kernel,</font>

8
00:00:31,999 --> 00:00:34,400
<font color="#000000">all validated on ninety datasets.</font>

9
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:40,794
<font color="#000000">Tree ensembles and neural networks are two of the
most widely used model families.</font>

10
00:00:40,874 --> 00:00:46,516
<font color="#000000">A soft tree is a decision tree whose splitting
rules are made differentiable,</font>

11
00:00:46,596 --> 00:00:51,793
<font color="#000000">so the whole model trains by gradient descent
instead of greedy search.</font>

12
00:00:51,873 --> 00:00:55,212
<font color="#000000">Ensembles of soft trees excel on tabular data,</font>

13
00:00:55,292 --> 00:01:01,306
<font color="#000000">and practitioners rely on tricks like parameter
sharing, adjusting split hardness,</font>

14
00:01:01,386 --> 00:01:03,238
<font color="#000000">and over-parameterization.</font>

15
00:01:03,318 --> 00:01:06,508
<font color="#000000">Yet almost no theory explains why they work.</font>

16
00:01:06,588 --> 00:01:08,440
<font color="#000000">This paper fills that gap.</font>

17
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:15,012
<font color="#000000">The Neural Tangent Kernel has become a powerful
tool for understanding neural networks</font>

18
00:01:15,092 --> 00:01:17,491
<font color="#000000">with infinitely many hidden nodes.</font>

19
00:01:17,571 --> 00:01:23,471
<font color="#000000">It has been derived for multi-layer perceptrons,
convolutional networks, and more,</font>

20
00:01:23,551 --> 00:01:26,242
<font color="#000000">each yielding its own distinct kernel.</font>

21
00:01:26,322 --> 00:01:29,815
<font color="#000000">But no one had derived an NTK for tree ensembles.</font>

22
00:01:29,895 --> 00:01:34,337
<font color="#000000">Because soft trees inherit characteristics of
neural networks,</font>

23
00:01:34,417 --> 00:01:38,275
<font color="#000000">the authors saw the NTK framework as the natural lens,</font>

24
00:01:38,355 --> 00:01:42,650
<font color="#000000">seeking a closed-form kernel for infinitely many
soft trees.</font>

25
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:46,616
<font color="#000000">The paper makes four contributions.</font>

26
00:01:46,696 --> 00:01:50,282
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

27
00:01:50,362 --> 00:01:53,947
<font color="#000000">it derives the Tree Neural Tangent Kernel at
initialization for infinitely many perfect</font>

28
00:01:54,028 --> 00:01:57,613
<font color="#000000">binary trees of arbitrary depth,</font>

29
00:01:57,693 --> 00:02:01,279
<font color="#000000">and proves the kernel stays essentially constant
during training,</font>

30
00:02:01,359 --> 00:02:04,945
<font color="#000000">enabling analysis as kernel regression and a proof
of global convergence.</font>

31
00:02:05,025 --> 00:02:08,611
<font color="#000000">Second, it shows oblivious ensembles,</font>

32
00:02:08,691 --> 00:02:12,277
<font color="#000000">which share splitting rules within each depth as in NODE,</font>

33
00:02:12,357 --> 00:02:15,943
<font color="#000000">converge to the very same kernel.</font>

34
00:02:16,023 --> 00:02:19,608
<font color="#000000">Third, it characterizes the decision function,</font>

35
00:02:19,688 --> 00:02:23,274
<font color="#000000">nearly linear in the basic case and more nonlinear
as splits harden.</font>

36
00:02:23,354 --> 00:02:26,940
<font color="#000000">Fourth, it uncovers a degeneracy where deep trees
flatten the kernel.</font>

37
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:30,629
<font color="#000000">The method takes the number of trees to infinity.</font>

38
00:02:30,709 --> 00:02:33,465
<font color="#000000">For perfect binary soft trees of depth d,</font>

39
00:02:33,545 --> 00:02:39,621
<font color="#000000">the authors prove the Tree Neural Tangent Kernel
converges to a deterministic kernel with</font>

40
00:02:39,701 --> 00:02:44,463
<font color="#000000">two parts: one from the inner splitting nodes and
one from the leaves.</font>

41
00:02:44,543 --> 00:02:47,507
<font color="#000000">The soft split uses a scaled error function,</font>

42
00:02:47,587 --> 00:02:50,896
<font color="#000000">a smooth sigmoid whose sharpness is set by alpha,</font>

43
00:02:50,976 --> 00:02:55,461
<font color="#000000">and its expectations have closed forms, so the
kernel is analytic.</font>

44
00:02:55,541 --> 00:02:59,335
<font color="#000000">Because the kernel stays constant and positive definite,</font>

45
00:02:59,415 --> 00:03:03,623
<font color="#000000">training reduces to kernel regression with global
convergence,</font>

46
00:03:03,703 --> 00:03:08,880
<font color="#000000">and the formula is not recursive in depth, so its
cost is depth-independent.</font>

47
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:12,261
<font color="#000000">The empirical study uses two kinds of data.</font>

48
00:03:12,341 --> 00:03:13,622
<font color="#000000">For generalization,</font>

49
00:03:13,702 --> 00:03:19,498
<font color="#000000">the authors run kernel regression with the Tree NTK
on ninety real-world datasets,</font>

50
00:03:19,578 --> 00:03:25,875
<font color="#000000">comparing against the kernel of an infinitely wide
multi-layer perceptron and the classic</font>

51
00:03:25,955 --> 00:03:27,953
<font color="#000000">radial basis function kernel.</font>

52
00:03:28,033 --> 00:03:31,321
<font color="#000000">Tree depth is tuned from one up to twenty-nine,</font>

53
00:03:31,401 --> 00:03:35,262
<font color="#000000">and the split-hardness parameter alpha is swept widely.</font>

54
00:03:35,342 --> 00:03:37,626
<font color="#000000">For measuring computational cost,</font>

55
00:03:37,706 --> 00:03:43,430
<font color="#000000">they use a synthetic dataset of three hundred
samples with ten Gaussian features.</font>

56
00:03:43,810 --> 00:03:46,688
<font color="#000000">The experiments confirm every prediction.</font>

57
00:03:46,768 --> 00:03:48,780
<font color="#000000">As the number of trees grows,</font>

58
00:03:48,860 --> 00:03:53,036
<font color="#000000">the empirical kernel converges to the closed-form
Tree NTK,</font>

59
00:03:53,116 --> 00:03:56,354
<font color="#000000">and training dynamics match kernel regression.</font>

60
00:03:56,434 --> 00:04:00,322
<font color="#000000">Making trees deeper first improves then hurts accuracy,</font>

61
00:04:00,402 --> 00:04:02,702
<font color="#000000">exactly the predicted degeneracy.</font>

62
00:04:02,782 --> 00:04:07,968
<font color="#000000">On the ninety datasets the multi-layer perceptron
kernel wins on average,</font>

63
00:04:08,048 --> 00:04:12,729
<font color="#000000">but the tree kernel is better on more than thirty
percent of them,</font>

64
00:04:12,809 --> 00:04:15,254
<font color="#000000">where the tree inductive bias fits.</font>

65
00:04:15,334 --> 00:04:20,520
<font color="#000000">And because its cost never grows with depth, it is
far faster to compute.</font>

66
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:22,723
<font color="#000000">Two ablations are central.</font>

67
00:04:22,803 --> 00:04:29,164
<font color="#000000">Sweeping the split-hardness parameter alpha shows
harder splits give better dataset-wise</font>

68
00:04:29,244 --> 00:04:31,579
<font color="#000000">win rates against the MLP kernel,</font>

69
00:04:31,659 --> 00:04:39,118
<font color="#000000">climbing from about fourteen percent when splits
are soft to nearly thirty-five percent at the hardest,</font>

70
00:04:39,198 --> 00:04:42,411
<font color="#000000">all above the radial basis function baseline.</font>

71
00:04:42,491 --> 00:04:46,217
<font color="#000000">Sweeping tree depth reproduces the degeneracy story.</font>

72
00:04:46,297 --> 00:04:51,633
<font color="#000000">The authors also verify empirically that oblivious
trees, as used by NODE,</font>

73
00:04:51,713 --> 00:04:55,000
<font color="#000000">induce the same kernel as ordinary soft trees.</font>

74
00:04:55,380 --> 00:04:57,693
<font color="#000000">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

75
00:04:57,773 --> 00:05:00,159
<font color="#000000">Across ninety real-world datasets,</font>

76
00:05:00,239 --> 00:05:06,250
<font color="#000000">the tree kernel beats the multi-layer perceptron
kernel on more than thirty percent,</font>

77
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:10,166
<font color="#000000">peaking near thirty-five percent when splits are hard,</font>

78
00:05:10,246 --> 00:05:14,880
<font color="#000000">versus under twelve percent for the radial basis
function kernel.</font>

79
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:19,739
<font color="#000000">Most striking, the tree kernel's cost is
independent of tree depth,</font>

80
00:05:19,819 --> 00:05:22,422
<font color="#000000">while the MLP kernel scales linearly.</font>

81
00:05:22,502 --> 00:05:25,540
<font color="#000000">The depths studied span one to twenty-nine.</font>

82
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:30,478
<font color="#000000">The lasting takeaway is that the neural tangent
kernel framework,</font>

83
00:05:30,558 --> 00:05:34,688
<font color="#000000">once used only for neural networks, applies to tree
models.</font>

84
00:05:34,768 --> 00:05:41,965
<font color="#000000">The resulting Tree NTK explains many soft-tree
behaviors from one theory: training converges globally,</font>

85
00:05:42,045 --> 00:05:45,176
<font color="#000000">oblivious sharing costs nothing in the limit,</font>

86
00:05:45,256 --> 00:05:48,886
<font color="#000000">deep trees degenerate into a nearly constant kernel,</font>

87
00:05:48,966 --> 00:05:52,811
<font color="#000000">and harder splits yield a more useful nonlinear kernel.</font>

88
00:05:52,891 --> 00:05:57,520
<font color="#000000">It also delivers a fast kernel whose cost is
independent of depth.</font>
